sections.s2_technical:
Engineers often reach for exponential backoff with jitter and believe they have made their retries safe. The timing strategy is genuinely valuable: without it, a thousand clients that all fail at the same instant will all retry at the same instant, creating a synchronised thundering herd that keeps the dependency pinned down. Jitter randomises the delays so the herd spreads out. But backoff answers exactly one question — the scheduling question — and leaves three more unanswered.

The first unanswered question is whether a retry should exist at all. If the operation is not idempotent, no amount of elegant spacing makes a second attempt safe; it just double-charges the customer a little later. If the error is permanent, like a validation failure or a four-hundred-level client error, retrying is pointless regardless of timing.

```java
// Backoff with jitter spaces attempts, but still retries a NON-idempotent charge → double charge
long delay = (long) (Math.pow(2, attempt) * 100) + rng.nextInt(100); // perfect jitter...
Thread.sleep(delay);
paymentApi.charge(card, amount); // ...still charges twice if the first one actually succeeded
```

The second unanswered question is who owns the decision. In a chain of gateway to service to client library, if every layer retries three times, you do not get three attempts, you get three times three times three, which is twenty-seven. Backoff at each layer spaces each layer's attempts but does nothing about the multiplication across layers. The ownership principle is that retries should live at exactly one layer, normally the one with the most context about the user's intent and deadline — usually the edge — while inner layers fail fast.

```java
// Ownership: ONE layer retries, inner layers fail fast → attempts add up to 3, not 27
public Result edgeCall(Request r) {
    return retrier.withBudget(() -> innerService.callNoRetry(r)); // inner layer does NOT retry
}
```

The third unanswered question is whether the dependency can afford the retries right now. This is the capacity-budget insight. Every retry is an additional request that consumes threads, connections, and CPU on the dependency. When a dependency is failing specifically because it is overloaded, retries are not resilience, they are the accelerant. The correct control is a retry budget: cap retries as a fraction of total traffic, for example "retries may never exceed ten percent of requests." When failures are widespread, the budget is exhausted almost immediately and the system stops amplifying, letting the dependency recover.

```java
// Retry budget: retries allowed only while under 10% of total request volume
class RetryBudget {
    private final double ratio; private long requests, retries;
    RetryBudget(double ratio) { this.ratio = ratio; }
    synchronized void recordRequest() { requests++; }
    synchronized boolean allowRetry() {
        if (retries + 1 > requests * ratio) return false; // budget spent → fail fast
        retries++; return true;
    }
}
```

The senior-level nuance is that backoff, jitter, idempotency, single-layer ownership, retry budgets, and circuit breakers are *complementary*, not substitutes. Backoff and jitter handle the timing; idempotency makes a retry correct; single-layer ownership stops multiplication; the budget caps total amplification; and the circuit breaker cuts retries entirely once the dependency is clearly down. A team that only adds backoff has solved the easiest quarter of the problem and often believes it has solved all of it, which is exactly why "we have exponential backoff with jitter" is not an answer to "is your retry policy safe."

